\section{Evaluation Methodology}
\label{sec:sim-and-eval}

\subsection{Sea-state ensemble and reference motion}

The estimator is evaluated on four stationary JONSWAP seas and four
Pierson--Moskowitz seas with nonlinear Stokes harmonics, spanning the conditions
in Table~\ref{tab:scenarios}.  Each record is \SI{1200}{s} long at
\SI{200}{Hz}.  The simulator provides reference three-dimensional displacement,
velocity, acceleration, and attitude together with body-frame specific force,
angular rate, and magnetic-field measurements.  The principal results are
scored over the final \SI{900}{s}; startup and one-time magnetic-reference
reconfiguration therefore occur before the common statistical window.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Stationary simulation scenarios.}
  \label{tab:scenarios}
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
    \toprule
    Scenario & $H_s$ [m] & $T_p$ [s] \\
    \midrule
    W1 & 0.27 & 3.0  \\
    W2 & 1.50 & 5.7  \\
    W3 & 4.00 & 8.5  \\
    W4 & 8.50 & 11.4 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

The stationary records are directional rather than scalar heave inputs.  The
medium-sea examples in Fig.~\ref{fig:input-spectra-3d} show the joint frequency--direction
energy density used to generate the kinematic reference.  The two spectral
families are kept separate in inference: the JONSWAP ensemble supplies the
declared primary comparison, while PM--Stokes is a second predeclared family
that tests whether the result survives a different spectral shape and bound
harmonics.

