\subsection{Sensor and model-error injection}

The inertial measurements include white noise, residual constant bias, and slow
bias drift.  The magnetometer additionally includes a hard-iron residual,
scale/cross-axis error, misalignment, and slow drift.  The principal levels are
summarized in Table~\ref{tab:sim_constants}.  The accelerometer truth retains a
random-walk bias while the estimator models the residual about its temperature
calibration as a slow first-order Gauss--Markov process, so the study deliberately
contains bias-process mismatch.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Principal simulation timing and sensor-error levels.}
  \label{tab:sim_constants}
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
    \toprule
    Quantity & Value & Notes \\
    \midrule
    IMU rate & \SI{200}{Hz} & prediction and accel update \\
    Magnetometer rate & \SI{25}{Hz} & asynchronous update \\
    $S$ pseudo-update period & $\tau$-scaled & \SIrange{5}{250}{ms} bounds \\
    Accel white noise & \SI{0.0148}{m.s^{-2}} & RMS per sample \\
    Accel bias RW & \SI{5e-4}{m.s^{-2}.s^{-0.5}} & truth model \\
    Gyro white noise & \SI{0.00157}{rad.s^{-1}} & RMS per sample \\
    Gyro bias RW & \SI{1e-5}{rad.s^{-1}.s^{-0.5}} & truth model \\
    Mag white noise & \SI{0.80}{\micro\tesla} & RMS per mag sample \\
    Mag hard-iron residual & \SI{2.0}{\micro\tesla} & half-range \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

Temperature is not varied in the scored simulations.  There is no independent
thermal calibration-error, lag, or hysteresis experiment, so the finite-$\tau_b$
temperature-centered residual is a structural sensor model and a stability
assumption, not a demonstrated temperature-compensation result.  The relevant
empirical question here is whether the estimator remains useful under mismatch
between that residual model and the injected random-walk bias.

The optional IMU lever-arm model is implemented but the principal study uses the
reference-point configuration committed with the validation bundle; it is not a
physical validation of an off-CoG installation.  Likewise, the records model a
surface-following inertial package rather than the response of a particular hull
through a vessel RAO.

\subsection{Paired Monte Carlo protocol}

Ten predeclared seed triplets independently vary wave phase, IMU noise/bias
realization, and sensor-error initialization.  A triplet is shared between
OU--II, OU--III, and each matched tuning ablation.  This pairing converts the
comparison from two independent ensembles into a distribution of within-seed
differences.

The declared primary endpoint is the seed-level mean normalized vertical
RMS displacement error across the four stationary JONSWAP seas.  Scenario
summaries report the sample mean and standard deviation across the ten seeds.
Paired effects use percentile-bootstrap intervals obtained by resampling the
seed-level paired differences; a Student-$t$ interval, exact sign test, and
exact paired sign-flip test are reported as companion checks for the single
primary endpoint.  Descriptive secondary comparisons retain paired intervals
without promoting each one to an additional confirmatory hypothesis.  A full
simulator generation establishes immutable replay provenance: replay source
commit, replay-producing implementation/build hashes, input hashes, and the
normalized raw-row hash.  A later statistical restatement is recorded
separately and is permitted only while that replay dependency closure and the
raw rows still match; it cannot substitute for a replay after implementation
changes.  The evidence contract enforces the same file-hash guarantees in a
source archive even when Git metadata is unavailable.

The reported metrics are not restricted to heave.  The study scores
$X$, $Y$, $Z$, and 3-D displacement RMS; roll, pitch, and yaw RMS; accelerometer
and gyroscope bias error; direction diagnostics; and the adaptation traces.
This is important for a three-dimensional estimator: improvement in $Z$ alone
is insufficient if it is purchased by uncontrolled surge, sway, or attitude
error.

\subsection{Adaptation and covariance ablations}
\label{par:fixed-points}
\label{par:channel-ablation}

Three principal operating modes separate adaptation from architecture.
\emph{Adaptive} runs the deployed online schedule.  \emph{FixedNominal} freezes
the operating point calibrated from a noise-free $H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$,
$T_p=\SI{5.7}{s}$ record.  \emph{FixedOracle} uses the same calibration
procedure for the sea being scored, or the known final sea of the transition;
it is a scenario-matched nondeployable reference, not an error-minimizing
oracle.

For OU--III, a $2\times2$ channel ablation further separates the OU process
parameters from the integral regularizer.  In \emph{$r_S$ only},
$(\tau,\sigma_{aw})$ are frozen while the regularizer follows the live tuner.
In \emph{OU only}, the OU parameters adapt while $r_S$ is fixed.  A matched
covariance-policy control repeats Adaptive and FixedNominal without the periodic
latent-acceleration covariance re-alignment.  These controls are necessary
because the filter contains several mechanisms that change simultaneously in a
naive adaptive-versus-fixed comparison.

\subsection{Controlled non-stationary case}

The changing-sea study is a $C^2$ quintic crossfade from
$(H_s,T_p)=(\SI{1.5}{m},\SI{5.7}{s})$ to
$(\SI{4.0}{m},\SI{11.4}{s})$ over $t=420$--$780$~s.  The endpoint records are
independently phase randomized, and derivative-consistent velocity and
acceleration include the terms introduced by the time-varying blend.  This is a
kinematically closed transition, not a continuously evolving wave spectrum.
Both endpoint spectra coexist during the blend, so an intermediate sample need
not have a unique $T_p$ and can be effectively bimodal.  Interpretation therefore
uses separate pure-start, blend, and pure-end intervals in addition to the
common trailing-window score.

The study consequently does not establish performance for arbitrary spectral
evolution, crossing seas, or a continuously changing directional spectrum.
Those are left as explicit extensions rather than folded into the claim made by
this controlled transition.
